cy to from a lower frequency to a  higher one the difference between this  sort of toy data and our senses as I  mentioned before is that our senses  don't have a particular starting  frequency to start with so it it's very  regularly so by doing these resampling  operations sometimes we're actually app  sampling if the sensor data is very  sparse or  depending whether it's very sparse or  very dense so we get very different  results across across the senses and so  what what we did here is a very simple  trick of doing this resampling in a  two-step process where we first up  sample all of our sensor data - to a  very high frequency let's say a minute  and then down sample again - to the  desired frequency that we actually want  to end up with and this works this works  actually very nice so in this case it  will look something like up sample  everything - a minute again use the mean  a3 sampling method interpolate linearly  and then because our target frequency is  seven minutes we down sample again - to  the target frequency and now we forward  so because we're going to from from from  high frequency that is one minute to two  to seven minutes and so apply it to to  actual data this is a temperature curve  the blue one as the original data and  then you see that the resample points in  this case piece in 15 minutes match the  the underlying data very well here's  here's another example of a noisy sensor  where you have these periods of of very  sensitive periods of those sensor  jumping between  - values and then you have these periods  of where the sensor is constant and  again here applying these resampling to  to this regular frequency works very  well and so the data that we end up with  this time series format essentially has  this this day time index and and and the  columns now are the sensors that we then  simply can join arbitrarily as we want  and work with them together so this is  this is what I wanted to say on on  resampling for us a very important 