 here's three strategies that we used  in order to approach this the first one  would be the direct step approach where  essentially you you train a model in  order to predict one step ahead and then  you train a completely separate model in  order to predict to two steps ahead and  so on and so forth so for every step  ahead that you want to predict you you  have a separate model the downside of  this of course is that you end up with  this computationally very heavy because  you for each new target for each step  you're training a new model and if you  extrapolate this to to all the rooms in  your building you end up with with with  a high number of models and the second  downside is that essentially you use  this predictive this the temporal  dependency between predicted values  because the prediction at T plus 2  doesn't know anything about the  prediction that keeps us 1 it was  trained and predicted completely  independent from each other so another  approach that you can take to to do this  is through three turret of steps and  what you do here is you learn a model to  predict the next step and and then you  use you predict that step and you use  that prediction and feed it back in into  the model at in order to predict the  next step and so on and so forth you do  this for as many steps as you want to go  forward so now we have only a single  model but the downside of this is  and that you're essentially accumulating  an error as you go into the future  because you're always using the  predicted value to feed it back in into  into to your models to predict the next  step so as you go forward the prediction  error goes up and so what we ended up  doing is the third strategy and that is  multi-layer prediction where we state  the problem the same but now we are not  trying to predict a binary binary value  but but an array of binary values where  this array essentially are all the  targets of the the steps I had that we  want to predict and so nice about this 